     "solution": "Request: drop. Only its client id and time matter; both are arguments\nUser / API key / IP: drop. The client id itself, a string; not modelled\nRule: keep. Capacity and refill rate, validated once when built; one value so per-plan rules come cheaply\nTokenBucket: keep. Tokens left and when it last refilled, for one client; it answers try_consume\nRateLimiter: keep. Holds the rule and one bucket per client; the entry point\nClock: drop. Dropped: the time is a number passed into each call\nResponse, HTTP 429: drop. Someone else's job; allow returns a pair",
     "why": "Three classes. Rule earns its class because it rejects a bad limit once, in one place, and a tier is another Rule. TokenBucket holds the two fields the refill needs.",
     "say": "I underline the nouns. **The limiter, the bucket and the rule stay.** A rule looks like two numbers, but it holds the check that they are positive, and a plan would swap in a different one. A request is a client id and a time, so it is two arguments. The clock goes away because the caller passes the time in."
