     "id": "ratelimiter-classes-w",
     "q": "Write each class as − state and + behaviour.",
     "starter": "class RateLimiter\n- \n+ \n\nclass TokenBucket\n- \n+ \n\nclass Rule\n- \n+ ",
     "hint": "The limiter holds a bucket per client. A bucket holds its tokens and when it last refilled. The rule holds the two numbers.",
     "solution": "class RateLimiter\n- rule: Rule  # req 3: every bucket follows it\n- buckets: Map<client_id, TokenBucket>  # req 2: one bucket per client, made on first use\n+ allow(client_id, now) -> (allowed: bool, retry_after: seconds) | error  # req 1: the one call a service makes\n+ _bucket_for(client_id, now) -> TokenBucket  # req 2: find or make the client's bucket\n\nclass TokenBucket\n- rule: Rule  # capacity to cap at, rate to refill at\n- tokens: float  # what is left to spend; fractional as it refills\n- last_refill: seconds  # refill by the time since this\n+ try_consume(now) -> (allowed, retry_after)  # req 1: take a token or say when to retry\n+ _refill(now) -> void  # req 2: tokens grow with time, up to capacity\n\nclass Rule\n- capacity: int  # req 3: the burst size\n- refill_per_sec: float  # req 3: the steady rate\n+ __post_init__() -> void | error  # req 3: reject a zero or negative rule when built",
     "why": "The limiter finds the bucket; the bucket decides. That one call, try_consume(now), is the seam: a sliding window class with the same call drops in without touching allow.",
     "say": "**I start from the methods and ask what each one has to read.** allow has to find a client's bucket, so the limiter keeps a map of them. try_consume has to know how many tokens are left and how long since the last refill, so those are the bucket's state, and it needs the capacity and the rate, which come from the Rule. Time is never state: it comes in as an argument."
